**Read:** [Inventory Analytics — Roberto Rossi](../sources/Inventory-Analytics-Rossi.lingua#r_61Y17EAC05XZSS1B28NX17SV7C), the complete stochastic and multi-echelon reasoning; [Graduate Supply Chain Planning — MIT 15.762J](../sources/Graduate-Supply-Chain-Planning-MIT.lingua#r_16MZ0PJM4KC10A3D9AYYESBT6P), the whole risk-pooling, placement and coordination programme; [Understanding Water Treatment Chemical Supply Chains and the Risk of Disruptions — EPA](../sources/Chemical-Supply-Chains-and-Disruption-Risk-EPA.lingua#r_6Y999Z6S9P5T6FY75NA2HGVW6C), the complete precursor and infrastructure dependency analyses.

Suppose supplier A is available with probability 0.85 and B with 0.90. If availability were independent and either could meet the full need, joint outage would be 0.15×0.10=0.015. But a joint table with probabilities 0.80 for both available, 0.05 for only A, 0.10 for only B and 0.05 for neither has those same marginals and a 0.05 joint outage. The identity and capacity of the common cause matter.

Explain a physical mechanism for dependence: a shared producer, raw material, terminal, utility, transport corridor or analytical service. Then distinguish this mechanism from correlation estimated from a short incomplete history. Qualified alternate capacity may also be too small, too late or incompatible with the receiving interface even when its producer remains available.

Build three tables with the same marginals but different joint behavior and compare the user's service. State what the data constrain and what is assumed. Investigate how independence of production, delivery and release differ. Use the actual supplier/route map to propose a meaningful diversification study, then connect it with inventory, substitute qualification and recovery work. A second seller is useful only through a demonstrated additional ability to supply the user's required material.

**Continue:** [Compare the reserve that can bridge an outage with the work needed to restore supply](Usable-reserve-time-restoration-time-and-service-loss.lingua#r_5XFMY0T36NG3KX9SZGGB44A1A9).

